The catastrophic elastic rebound phenomenon in Antarctica, driven by the 1000x drop in mantle viscosity, creates an environment where vertical displacement rates exceed 10–40 meters per year in central regions (with initial displacement exceeding 15 m/yr and stabilizing to 4–5 m/yr after a decade). Over a 30-year planning window, this results in net elevation changes of 300–500+ meters above the current mean sea level baseline.

Given that:
1.  Individual GNSS anchor stations may need vertical adjustment every few months to maintain sub-meter accuracy;
2.  The format of existing coordinate reference frames (typically static earth-fixed ellipsoidal heights) is intrinsically incompatible with a continuously moving datum;
3.  Software and user appliances worldwide are calibrated against datasets that will become invalid within years, not decades;

I would recommend **(B)** the company should budget for developing **new dynamic coordinate systems** and **much more frequent global recalibration procedures**. The failure to do so would result in systemic navigational errors that compound rapidly, potentially causing cumulative position drift of tens to hundreds of meters per year for any long-term user near active rebound zones.
